    "acting_helped": "yes. Both published levels fired, in the awkward order, and the trade still worked. The ADD line at 63,800 fired first on fully valid mechanics - the 14:00Z 1H bar settled 63,862.0, 62.0 points above the line, with aggregate BTC open interest falling from $2,688,568,878 to $2,679,314,772 across that same bar - and two bars later price traded 62,846.3, 1,015.7 points lower. The REDUCE line at 63,309.4 then fired on the 16:00Z bar, which settled 63,165.1, a genuine settle 144.3 points through it. A desk obeying both triggers mechanically would have bought 63,862.0 and sold 63,165.1 inside two hours for -697 points, which is the real lesson of the session: composite triggers firing on a spike into a level, inside a range that is actively breaking, produce the worst available fills. The TRADE is the positive and it is the first winner in six sessions: the 17% spot trim executed at 63,476.3 marks at 63,273.4, 202.9 points in front, +0.32% on the trimmed notional. It survived a 523.6-point adverse excursion to the 63,999.9 session high before working."
