    "acting_helped": "no. Both of morning's levels fired within four hours of each other and both fired on their published mechanics. The reduce line at 62,685.7 went first: the 13:00Z 1H bar settled 62,656.6, 29.1 points through, and the 14:00Z bar then traded to 62,521.8, the session low and 163.9 points below morning's own low. The add line at 62,846.3 fired next, on the 15:00Z settle at 62,984.7, 138.4 points through - though its open-interest condition could not be evaluated, because the only OI readings available at the time morning ran were cached bodies 16 to 23 hours old, so there is no honest baseline to difference against. The trade is the negative. Morning trimmed 20 percent of the spot book at 62,874.9 and spot marks 170.1 points higher at 63,045.0, so the sold notional is offside by 0.27 percent, roughly 0.04 USDT. What makes this instructive rather than merely unlucky is the shape: the trim was RIGHT for two hours, with a maximum favourable excursion of 353.1 points to the low, and then the market reversed 722.8 points off that low in three hours and took it offside. Eleven consecutive sessions now show the same split - the levels are accurate and the executions lose - and the mechanism is finally legible. The desk places its exit AT the level, and the level is precisely where the reversal happens, because that is where the liquidations that cause reversals are clustered. Midday's response is to stop treating a reduce as a directional call and start treating it as half of a pair: reduce at the level, re-add on the reclaim. This session is the first data point in that test."
